{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794442047,"nonce":"l0t9gLieytpDRm0pX6/8+J/1yZk=","message":"Committed as b7d3c89, not pushed.\n\nItem 2, the part left out before: `async fn`, and `Future\u003c()\u003e`.\n\nAn async function is read as a function returning `Future\u003cT\u003e` of what it gives (`Method::asynchronous`; `fallible` is then whether it gives a `Result`, whose error fails the future). The Haxe classes and the manifest see a function that returns a future; the manifest adds `\"async\": true` and writes `\"fallible\": false`, since the call itself does not fail with the library's error.\n\nThe earlier objection was that an adapter cannot hold `\u0026self` or a `\u0026str` across an await. It does not have to: `spawn(async move { ... })` owns what the work uses.\n- numbers and `bool`: converted before the work starts, so a number out of range still fails the call\n- `\u0026str`, `\u0026[u8]`: copied (`String::from`, `to_vec`) and passed by reference from inside the work\n- a shared interface, as `\u0026T`, `Arc\u003cT\u003e` or an `Option` of either: a clone of the `Arc`\n- the receiver: `self` of an interface that is not shared (taken out, so the Haxe object is closed at once), or `\u0026self` of a shared one (a clone of the `Arc`)\n\nRefused, each with the reason that the work runs after the call has returned: `\u0026self` or `\u0026mut self` of an interface that is not shared, a borrowed object that is not shared, a cell, a lent function, `new`, `#[hxn(blocking)]`, returning a `Future` itself, and a shared interface not in its `Arc`.\n\nNothing carried: `Future\u003c()\u003e` and `Stream\u003c()\u003e` are taken now, since an async function that returns nothing is the common case. Over the boundary it is a flag that is always set, taken like a bool; in Haxe it is `hxn.Unit`, an enum of one value, `Nothing`. `Future\u003cVoid\u003e` was tried first and does not work for a caller: `() -\u003e Void` does not unify with `(Void) -\u003e Void`, so `then(() -\u003e ...)` is refused. `Signal\u003c()\u003e` is refused.\n\nFixture: `Later::squared`, `shout`, `settle`, `agree`, `counted`, `ticks`; `Counter::into_value_later`; `Pot::add_later`, `pour`. Generated.hx checks that a consumed counter is closed at once and released when the work ends, and that a shared value outlives the object that started work on it.\n\nNot built:\n- A number (`u8`, `i64`, ...) or bytes given by an async function or carried by a future: what a future carries is unchanged but for `()`.\n- An async function whose future is not `Send` is a compile error in the adapter, with rustc's message and not one of hxn's.\n- `\u0026self` of an interface that is not shared. It would need the handle borrowed for as long as the work runs.\n\nRun: tests/counter/run.sh gen-hl on Ash (hybrid, jit and interp, three times each) and on stock HashLink on the NUC (and three more times), gen-cpp, gen-cppia, gen-wasm, gen-emcc; the hand-written hl, cpp and emcc modes; tests/cli/run.sh on Ash and hxcpp; tests/examples/run.sh on Ash. The hand-written wasm mode still traps in the local Ash build, as noted on the wasm issue, with or without this change. Not run: Windows and hxcpp on Linux, which CI does on push.","files":null}]}